# bnbcode findings — continual work, context growth, and agent stalls

Field notes from running **bnbcode 0.50.0.dev24** as a long-horizon research agent, with
the raw evidence to check them against. Written to be handed to the bnbcode authors.

Everything here is measured unless a paragraph says otherwise; where a claim is an
inference from the measurements, it says so.

---

## The setup

Three mathematical-optimisation problems (autocorrelation-inequality bounds, Erdős minimum
overlap), each handed to a coding agent as a self-contained folder with the task in
`INITIAL_PROMPT.md`, an evaluator, and a starting `.npy`. The agent is asked to search for
better constructions for hours, keeping a ledger of what it tried.

- **Model:** `Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8`, self-hosted on vLLM 0.25.1, `--max-model-len 130000`,
  `--enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser qwen3_xml --reasoning-parser qwen3`.
  Not a frontier model, and that matters for reading finding 3.
- **bnbcode config:** `compaction.context_limit: 110000`, `compaction.notes: false`,
  `agent.build.temperature: 0.6`, `top_p: 0.95`, a permission block that denies web access.
- **Control arm:** the identical task, prompt, model and sampling driven by **Claude Code
  2.1.234** through a LiteLLM shim (Anthropic Messages → OpenAI). Same vLLM process. This
  exists to separate "the model is unreliable" from "the harness makes it worse".
- 11 bnbcode sessions across 3 run folders; 1 Claude Code run. Small-n, one operator.

---

## Finding 1 — continual work re-injects the entire user prompt on every continuation turn

**This is the main finding.** While a continual-work mode is active, the runtime inserts a
user-role message before continuation turns. That message is not a short nudge. It is:

```
<continual-work-north-star>
  … the ~500-character north_star …
</continual-work-north-star>

This is a direction, not a log. Use files, todos, and the codebase itself to
track progress and intermediate state. …

++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
The following recent user messages must be interpreted with the north_star
direction above in mind. …
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
<recent-genuine-user-messages>
  … THE COMPLETE ORIGINAL PROMPT, VERBATIM …
</recent-genuine-user-messages>
```

Measured on session `ac2_evo_s1__ses_fac771e7effe3pOzSfM05IBIUo`:

| | |
|---|---|
| one injection block | **16,360 characters** |
| the user's `INITIAL_PROMPT.md` | 10,703 characters |
| of that prompt embedded verbatim in the block | **100%** |
| the harness's own wrapper text | 5,658 characters |
| injections in this one session | **38** |
| distinct md5 among those 38 | **5** |
| total characters of injection | **572,579** (≈138,000 Qwen tokens) |

`evidence/continual_work_injection_FULL.txt` is one complete block, unedited.
`evidence/continual_work_injections_index.txt` lists all 38 with timestamps, sizes and
hashes.

### What that does to the context

Tokenised with Qwen's own tokenizer, everything accumulated in each session, by category:

| category | **bnbcode** `ac2_evo_s1` | **Claude Code** `ac2_evo_cc_s1` |
|---|---|---|
| **user / injected text** | **138,091 (68%)** | 15,973 (3%) |
| tool calls (the request) | 27,712 (14%) | 155,052 (32%) |
| assistant reasoning | 27,403 (13%) | 178,409 (37%) |
| tool results (the output) | 8,222 (4%) | 52,095 (11%) |
| attachments | — | 78,156 (16%) |
| assistant text | 1,632 (1%) | 1,434 (0%) |
| **total** | **203,060** | **481,119** |

Same task, same prompt variant, same model, roughly the same wall-clock window.

Two-thirds of what bnbcode accumulates is one message repeated. The evidence the agent
actually needs to reason about — the output of the programs it ran — is **4%**. In the
control arm the proportions are inverted: the work is ~80% of the context and the
harness's own text is 3%.

The agent also reasons three times less per turn under bnbcode: **347 tokens per reasoning
block versus 1,031** in the control arm.

### Why we think this matters (inference, not measurement)

A model shown forty near-identical copies of an instruction it has already acted on is in
a context that invites "this is handled" as the next token. That is consistent with
finding 3 but we have not isolated it experimentally. The next run disables continual work
with everything else held constant, which will settle it.

### Suggestion

Send the north_star and a short continuation instruction. If recent user messages need to
be restated, restate a summary or a reference, not the entire original prompt, and not on
every turn. At minimum, deduplicate: 33 of the 38 blocks were byte-identical to one of five
variants, so the same text was re-sent dozens of times inside one context window.

