## Finding 2 — the auto-disable safety bound fires on model unreliability, silently

From the binary's own `continue_work` description:

> "As a safety bound, the runtime also disables a mode after **three consecutive
> mode-prompt cycles repeat the same activity**."

That rule is aimed at an agent looping on completed work. What it actually caught here was
a *model* failing to emit tool calls. Three consecutive turns where Qwen thought
"let me continue optimizing" and emitted nothing were read as repeated activity, and
continual work switched itself off.

Observed on **3 of 3** bnbcode run folders. Session metadata before and after, from the
`session` table:

```
ses_facbe99c3…  (pre-compaction)   "mode": "optimization",  northStar: "Maximize AC2…"
ses_fac6a6e55…  (live)             "mode": "disabled",      northStar: gone
```

`ac2_evo_s1` reached the same state without ever compacting, so compaction is not the
mechanism — the safety bound is.

Two problems from an operator's seat:

1. **It is invisible.** Nothing in the TUI said continual work had been disabled. The
   symptom is "the agent stopped continuing", and the operator's response — nudge it again
   — cannot work, because the machinery that generates continuations is off. We only found
   it by reading `session.metadata` in postgres.
2. **The north_star is discarded with it.** The successor session has no `northStar` key,
   so re-enabling means the model has to reconstruct the direction from a compacted
   context.

### Suggestion

Surface the transition in the UI, and distinguish "the agent repeated completed work" from
"the agent produced no tool call three times" — the second is a provider/model
reliability event, and disabling the continuation machinery is close to the opposite of
the useful response. Keep the north_star across a disable so re-enabling is one call.

