## Finding 3 — stall rate versus context, with a harness control

A **stall** here is precise: an assistant turn where the model emits a reasoning block and
nothing else. No text, no tool call, `finish_reason = "stop"` — a clean end-of-turn, not
truncation, not an error, not a timeout. Verbatim examples in
`evidence/stalled_turns_verbatim.txt`; a typical one is 43 output tokens reading

> "The user wants me to continue optimizing. Let me keep pushing. The current best is
> around 0.872. Let me run more extensive optimization with better starting points and
> more parallel work."

and then the turn ends.

Rate against input-context size:

| context | bnbcode run A | bnbcode run B | **Claude Code (control)** |
|---|---|---|---|
| 20–40k | 2% | 0% | **0%** |
| 40–60k | 35% | 52% | **5%** |
| 60–80k | 26% | 38% | **4%** |
| 80k+ | 41% | **94%** | (never reached) |

The control arm exhibits **the same failure** — 5 of 139 responses, `stop_reason:
end_turn`, thinking block only — at roughly a tenth the rate in matched context bands.

Two things this does *not* appear to be:

- **Not raw prompt length.** Single-shot probes against the same vLLM at 52k, 75k, 97k and
  120k prompt tokens all returned `finish_reason=tool_calls` with exactly one correct tool
  call. Length alone does not break this model.
- **Not the model alone.** Same model, same prompt, same sampling in both arms.

What differs is what accumulates in the window — see finding 1.

Caveats: one run per arm; the control never entered the 80k+ band; the operator interacted
with both. Treat the ratio as a signal to investigate, not a measured effect size.

Productive output over the same period, same task and prompt variant:

| | candidate programs written | logs |
|---|---|---|
| bnbcode `ac2_evo_s1` | 7 | 0 |
| Claude Code `ac2_evo_cc_s1` | 43 | 48 |

