## Finding 4 — the shell tool's timeout, and how much wall-clock it costs

The model settled on passing `timeout: 900000` to the shell tool. The task's programs are
specified to run for up to 1000 s, so they are killed before returning:

```
<shell_metadata> shell tool terminated command after exceeding timeout 900000 ms. …
```

On one node, over 217 shell calls: **19 killed at the timeout, consuming 285 of 511
minutes of shell wall-clock — 56%.** Every one of those returned nothing to the model.

This is arguably correct behaviour given the argument the model chose, and there is no
clamp in the binary that we could find. Worth noting anyway, because the tool's error text
advises "retry with a larger timeout value" while the model is already passing the largest
value it has ever tried, and because a killed command hands the model a turn where acting
produced no information — which is exactly the state that precedes a stall.

Observation, not a complaint: `background: true` exists and the model discovered it on its
own 32 times. It is a good escape hatch; it is just not what the error message points at.

